{"name":"Dusty Duchess","hex":"#b18377","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"dusty","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Covered with dust.","Powdery and resembling dust."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A medium-brown color.","An old bottle of spirits that has been kept for a long time."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/dusty"},{"term":"duchess","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The wife or widow of a duke.","The female ruler of a duchy."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To court or curry favour for political or business advantage; to flatter obsequiously."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/duchess"}],"entries":[{"title":"Duke","blocks":[{"text":"Duke is a male title either of a monarch ruling over a duchy, or of a member of royalty, or nobility. As rulers, dukes are ranked below emperors, kings, grand princes, grand dukes, and above sovereign princes. As royalty or nobility, they are ranked below grand dukes and above or below princes, depending on the country or specific title. The title comes from French duc, itself from the Latin dux, 'leader', a term used in republican Rome to refer to a military commander without an official rank, and later coming to mean the leading military commander of a province. In most countries, the word duchess is the female equivalent."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/James_Brydges%2C_1st_Duke_of_Chandos_by_Michael_Dahl.jpg/330px-James_Brydges%2C_1st_Duke_of_Chandos_by_Michael_Dahl.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Duke"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Birger_jarl_%28Forss%C3%A9n%29.jpg/250px-Birger_jarl_%28Forss%C3%A9n%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d5/Birger_jarl_%28Forss%C3%A9n%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Birger_jarl_%28Forss%C3%A9n%29.jpg/250px-Birger_jarl_%28Forss%C3%A9n%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"In the 1260s Birger Jarl bore a ducal coronet and used the Latin title of Dux Sweorum (Latin for \"Duke of the Swedes\"); the design of his coronet combined those used by continental European and English dukes.[7]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABirger_jarl_(Forss%C3%A9n).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/Bengt_Birgersson.JPG/500px-Bengt_Birgersson.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/Bengt_Birgersson.JPG/1280px-Bengt_Birgersson.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/58/Bengt_Birgersson.JPG/500px-Bengt_Birgersson.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Bishop Bengt Birgersson (1254–1291) was the first Duke of Finland[8]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABengt_Birgersson.JPG"}],"sounds":{"dusty":[{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6c/En-us-dusty.ogg?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/6/6c/En-us-dusty.ogg/En-us-dusty.ogg.mp3"}],"duchess":[{"type":"audio/wav","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/24/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-duchess.wav?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/2/24/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-duchess.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-duchess.wav.mp3"},{"type":"a